  "nameStory": "The \"great\" in Great St. John's Wort refers to its impressive size - it's the giant among New England's St. John's worts, towering over its smaller cousins. Like all St. John's worts, it's named after St. John the Baptist because these plants traditionally bloom around his feast day in late June. The name \"wort\" is an old English word simply meaning \"plant,\" so this is literally \"St. John's plant.\"",
  "description": "Great St. John's Wort is a tall, robust perennial wildflower that lives up to its name as the largest St. John's wort in New England. This impressive plant can reach 3-6 feet tall, standing head and shoulders above its relatives. It produces clusters of bright yellow flowers with five petals and numerous prominent stamens that create a cheerful, sunny display. The sturdy stems and opposite leaves give it a substantial, almost shrub-like appearance despite being an herbaceous plant.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Flowers",
      "description": "Bright golden-yellow flowers are notably large for a St. John's wort, often 2 inches across, with five rounded petals and a conspicuous burst of long yellow stamens in the center that feel slightly sticky to the touch",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth Habit",
      "description": "Exceptionally tall for a St. John's wort, reaching 3-6 feet high with sturdy, upright stems that feel firm and substantial when gently squeezed",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Leaves",
      "description": "Large oval leaves arranged in opposite pairs along the stem, feeling smooth and slightly waxy, with tiny translucent dots visible when held up to sunlight",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Fruit/Seeds",
      "description": "Develops into egg-shaped seed capsules that split open to release small dark seeds, making a subtle rattling sound when shaken in late summer",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Great St. John's Wort thrives in moist, rich soils along stream banks, pond edges, and wet meadows throughout New England. It particularly favors partially shaded areas where it can get morning sun but afternoon protection, often growing alongside sedges, ferns, and other moisture-loving plants. You'll most likely spot it in low-lying areas that stay consistently damp but aren't completely waterlogged.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "New growth emerges as reddish shoots in late spring, with opposite leaves quickly unfurling along sturdy stems that grow rapidly in height.",
    "summer": "Peak blooming occurs in midsummer with showy yellow flowers lasting several weeks, followed by the development of distinctive egg-shaped seed capsules.",
    "fall": "Leaves may turn yellowish before dying back, while dried seed capsules persist and can be heard rattling in the wind when mature seeds are ready for dispersal.",
    "winter": "Dies back completely to ground level, leaving no visible trace above ground until spring growth begins again."
  },
  "funFact": "Great St. John's Wort is so rare in New England that it's been spotted only 27 times by naturalists - making it more elusive than many orchids and about as uncommon as finding a needle in a haystack of more typical wildflowers!",
  "confusionNotes": "Its impressive height immediately distinguishes it from other St. John's worts - if you're looking at a St. John's wort that reaches your waist or chest, you've likely found the great one. Remember: \"Great size, great St. John's wort.\"",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite its rarity in New England, Great St. John's Wort is more common in the Midwest and western regions, making New England sightings particularly special for botanists and wildflower enthusiasts.",
  "compareHints": "Great St. John's Wort dwarfs all other New England St. John's worts - Common St. John's Wort rarely exceeds 2-3 feet, while most others stay well under 2 feet tall. Its flowers are also notably larger and more showy than its relatives, and unlike the weedy Common St. John's Wort with its narrow leaves, Great St. John's Wort has broad, oval leaves that give it a more substantial appearance.",
